:: NPC offer nursery help [bg-nursery]
<h1 class="ptitle">NURSERY</h1>
<br /><br />
/* The player offers a caretaker a hand. Opened from the NPC view; choosing a task uses up this caretaker's current time slot. */
<<set _help = setup.nurseryEncounters>>
/* The NPC view works on $tmpGirl and saves it over the real record on the way out, so both have to be the same object here */
<<if typeof $slaveId !== 'undefined'>>
	<<set $slaves[$slaveId] = $tmpGirl>>
<<else>>
	<<set $guests[$guestId] = $tmpGirl>>
<</if>>
<<set _carer = $tmpGirl>>
<<set _backPassage = (typeof $slaveId !== 'undefined') ? 'NPC view' : 'NPC view - guest'>>
<<set _name = setup.displayName(_carer)>>
<<set _heOrShe = setup.pronounceWhat(_carer)>>
<<set _hisOrHer = setup.pronounceWhos(_carer)>>
<<set _himOrHer = setup.pronounceWho(_carer)>>
<<set _care = $player.nurseryCare>>
<<set _canPrep = timeBetween('08:00', '08:45') && ($player.quests.school_built ?? false) && setup.nurseryCare.kidsAged(5, 17).length > 0>>
<<set _canBedtime = timeBetween('19:00', '20:45')>>
<<set _canDrink = timeBetween('08:00', '19:00')>>
<<set _toyNeeds = { plastic: 1, cloth: 1, glue: 1, wood: 1 }>>
<<set _snackNeeds = { food: 2, plastic: 1 }>>
<<set _toyMissing = _help.missing(_toyNeeds)>>
<<set _snackMissing = _help.missing(_snackNeeds)>>
<<set _extra = setup.nurseryHelp>>
<<set _canTutor = _extra.tutoringOffered()>>
<<set _tutorBlocked = _extra.tutorBlockedBy()>>

You catch <<=_name>> between one small crisis and the next and ask if <<=_heOrShe>> has a moment. <<=ucfirst(_heOrShe)>> pushes a loose strand of hair back and looks at you warily, as if waiting to hear what has gone wrong.
<br /><br />
<<you>>
	Nothing's wrong. I wanted to say that I see how much work you put in here. These kids are better off because of you, and I appreciate it.
<</you>>
<br />
It takes a second to land. Then <<=_hisOrHer>> shoulders come down and <<=_heOrShe>> smiles, a little embarrassed.
<br /><br />
<<sayNpc _carer>>
	Oh. Thank you. People don't usually stop to say that.
<</sayNpc>>
<br />
<<you>>
	I mean it. So what can I do to help?
<</you>>
<br />
<<=ucfirst(_heOrShe)>> laughs, then sees that you're serious and takes a look around the room.
<br /><br />
<<sayNpc _carer>>
	Thank you, really. Now that you mention it, there are a few things you could do that would lighten my day.
<</sayNpc>>
<br />
<<=ucfirst(_heOrShe)>> counts them off on <<=_hisOrHer>> fingers, and you could help in any of these ways:
<br /><br />

<div id="nce_choices">
	<<if _canPrep>>
		<<link 'Help with school prep (Uses the time until 9am)'>>
			<<replace '#nce_choices'>>
				<<run _help.markHelped(_carer)>>
				<<set _mins = 60 - $gameDate.getMinutes()>>
				<<set _low = _mins >= 30 ? 2 : 1>>
				<<set _high = _mins >= 30 ? 4 : 2>>
				<<set _ownStudents = setup.nurseryCare.kidsAged(5, 17).filter(k => _help.isOwnChild(k))>>
				<<addmins _mins>>
				<<set _dBehavior = _help.change(_care, 'behavior', 3, 5)>>
				<<set _dHappy = _help.change(_carer, 'happy', _low, _high)>>
				<<set _dRel = _help.change(_carer, 'relationship', _low, _high)>>
				<<set _bonds = []>>
				<<for _student range _ownStudents>>
					<<run _bonds.push([_student.name, _help.changeBond(_student, _low, _high)])>>
				<</for>>
				<<if _mins >= 30>>
					You roll up your sleeves and work your way down the line with <<=_name>>: faces to wash, shoes on the wrong feet, a shirt on inside out, and one argument about whose coat is whose that takes both of you to settle. By the time the last of them is out the door, the room is quiet and nobody has left anything behind.
				<<else>>
					There isn't much time left, so you pitch in wherever you can. You tie a few laces, find a missing coat, and hurry the stragglers along. The last of them makes it out the door just in time.
				<</if>>
				<<if _ownStudents.length === 1>>
					<br /><br />
					<<=_ownStudents[0].name>> keeps glancing back at you all the way out the door, looking pleased that you came.
				<<elseif _ownStudents.length > 1>>
					<br /><br />
					Your own children keep glancing back at you all the way out the door, looking pleased that you came.
				<</if>>
				<br /><br />
				<<=_name>> drops onto the nearest cot and lets out a long breath.
				<br /><br />
				<<sayNpc _carer>>
					That is the smoothest morning we've had in a long time. Thank you. They behave better when they can see someone in charge cares enough to show up.
				<</sayNpc>>
				<br />
				<<link 'Continue'>><<goto _backPassage>><</link>>
				<<linkdesc>><<=_extra.effects([['Happiness', _dHappy], ['Relationship', _dRel], ['Behavior', _dBehavior]])>><</linkdesc>>
				<<for _bond range _bonds>>
					<<linkdesc>><<=_extra.effects([['Relationship', _bond[1]]], _bond[0])>><</linkdesc>>
				<</for>>
			<</replace>>
		<</link>>
	<</if>>
	<<if _canBedtime>>
		<<link 'Help get the kids settled for bed (Uses 1 hour, or the time until 9pm)'>>
			<<replace '#nce_choices'>>
				<<run _help.markHelped(_carer)>>
				<<run _help.startLog()>>
				<<set _mins = Math.min(60, 21 * 60 - ($gameDate.getHours() * 60 + $gameDate.getMinutes()))>>
				<<set _low = _mins >= 30 ? 2 : 1>>
				<<set _high = _mins >= 30 ? 4 : 2>>
				<<set _sleepers = _extra.kidsPresent()>>
				<<set _ownSleepers = _sleepers.filter(k => _help.isOwnChild(k))>>
				<<addmins _mins>>
				<<run setup.nurseryBehavior.change(3, 5)>>
				<<run _help.change(_carer, 'happy', _low, _high)>>
				<<run _help.change(_carer, 'relationship', _low, _high)>>
				<<for _sleeper range _sleepers>>
					<<run _help.change(_sleeper, 'happy', 2, 4)>>
					<<run _help.changeBond(_sleeper, 2, 4)>>
				<</for>>
				<<if _mins >= 30>>
					You take one end of the room and <<=_name>> takes the other. There are teeth to brush, faces to wipe, and one last trip to the outhouse that somehow turns into four. You tuck in blankets, check under a cot for monsters, and find a missing sock that someone insists they can't sleep without. One by one the lamps go down, and the room fills with the slow breathing of children who are finally asleep.
				<<else>>
					There isn't much of the evening left, so you pitch in wherever you can. You round up the last few stragglers, tuck in a couple of blankets, and turn down the lamps while <<=_name>> settles the ones who are still whispering.
				<</if>>
				<<if _ownSleepers.length === 1>>
					<br /><br />
					<<=_ownSleepers[0].name>> holds on to your hand a little longer than <<=setup.pronounceWhat(_ownSleepers[0])>> needs to when you tuck <<=setup.pronounceWho(_ownSleepers[0])>> in, and is still smiling when <<=setup.pronounceWhos(_ownSleepers[0])>> eyes close.
				<<elseif _ownSleepers.length > 1>>
					<br /><br />
					Your own children make sure you tuck each of them in, one after the other, and they are still smiling when their eyes close.
				<</if>>
				<br /><br />
				<<=_name>> sinks into the chair by the door and lets <<=_hisOrHer>> head fall back against the wall.
				<br /><br />
				<<sayNpc _carer>>
					Listen to that. Quiet. Most nights it takes me until long after dark to get them all down, and I'm too tired to enjoy it by the time I do. Thank you for staying.
				<</sayNpc>>
				<br />
				<<link 'Continue'>><<goto _backPassage>><</link>>
				<<nurseryEffects>>
			<</replace>>
		<</link>>
	<</if>>
	/* During school prep, the longer jobs wait until the school crowd is out the door */
	<<if !_canPrep>>
	<<link 'Help keep the kids occupied (Uses 1 hour)'>>
		<<replace '#nce_choices'>>
			<<run _help.markHelped(_carer)>>
			<<addmins 60>>
			<<set _dHappy = _help.change(_carer, 'happy', 3, 5)>>
			<<set _dRel = _help.change(_carer, 'relationship', 3, 5)>>
			<<set _dBehavior = _help.change(_care, 'behavior', 4, 8)>>
			<<set _standout = _extra.rollExemplary()>>
			<<set _inRoom = _extra.kidsPresent()>>
			<<set _ownInRoom = _inRoom.filter(k => _help.isOwnChild(k)).length>>
			<<if _ownInRoom === 1>>
				<<set _fatherOf = 'one'>>
			<<elseif _ownInRoom * 2 > _inRoom.length>>
				<<set _fatherOf = 'most'>>
			<<elseif _ownInRoom * 2 === _inRoom.length>>
				<<set _fatherOf = 'half'>>
			<<elseif _ownInRoom > 1>>
				<<set _fatherOf = 'some'>>
			<<else>>
				<<set _fatherOf = ''>>
			<</if>>
			You spend the next hour as <<=_name>>'s shadow, getting a taste of a day in the life taking care of the nursery. Some of it is play: a game of tag that sprawls across half the room, a tower of blocks that has to be built taller than you are, a story told three times because nobody liked the ending. The rest is keeping order. You break up a shoving match, move a few troublemakers to opposite ends of the room, and wait until a sulker is ready to say sorry.
			<br /><br />
			The kids notice who is standing beside <<=_name>>, and your status as the leader of the settlement<<if _fatherOf>>, and father to <<=_fatherOf>> of them,<</if>> doesn't go unnoticed. With you there, a single word or look settles most problems before they start, and the room gets a little quieter as the hour goes on. By the end of it, things run more smoothly than when you came in, at least for now.
			<br /><br />
			<<sayNpc _carer>>
				I could get used to that. They listen to you. I don't know if it's the voice or the size of you, but they listen.
			<</sayNpc>>
			<br />
			<<if _standout>>
				<<set _standoutHe = setup.pronounceWhat(_standout)>>
				<<set _standoutHim = setup.pronounceWho(_standout)>>
				<<set _standoutHis = setup.pronounceWhos(_standout)>>
				One of them stood out the whole time. <<=_standout.name>> did as <<=_standoutHe>> was asked without complaint, shared without being told to, and even helped one of the smaller ones pick up the mess.
				<br /><br />
				<div id="nce_standout">
					<<if _care.snacks > 0>>
						<<link `'Reward ' + _standoutHim + ' with a snack (Uses 1 nursery snack)'`>>
							<<set _care.snacks-->>
							<<set _dKidHappy = _help.change(_standout, 'happy', 3, 5)>>
							<<set _dKidRel = _help.change(_standout, 'relationship', 3, 5)>>
							<<set _dBehavior += _help.change(_care, 'behavior', 1, 3)>>
							<<replace '#nce_standout'>>
								You call <<=_standout.name>> over and, in front of everyone, tell <<=_standoutHim>> that you noticed how well <<=_standoutHe>> behaved today. Then you hand <<=_standoutHim>> a snack from the nursery stores. <<=_standout.name>> lights up and holds it in both hands like a trophy.
								<br /><br />
								The rest of the room saw it too. For the next little while, there's a good deal of very visible sharing and tidying going on.
								<br /><br />
								<<link 'Continue'>><<goto _backPassage>><</link>>
								<<linkdesc>><<=_extra.effects([['Happiness', _dHappy], ['Relationship', _dRel], ['Behavior', _dBehavior]])>><</linkdesc>>
								<<linkdesc>><<=_extra.effects([['Happiness', _dKidHappy], ['Relationship', _dKidRel]], _standout.name)>><</linkdesc>>
							<</replace>>
						<</link>>
					<<else>>
						<div>Reward <<=_standoutHim>> with a snack [No snacks in the nursery]</div>
					<</if>>
					<<link 'Continue'>><<goto _backPassage>><</link>>
					<<linkdesc>><<=_extra.effects([['Happiness', _dHappy], ['Relationship', _dRel], ['Behavior', _dBehavior]])>><</linkdesc>>
				</div>
			<<else>>
				<<link 'Continue'>><<goto _backPassage>><</link>>
				<<linkdesc>><<=_extra.effects([['Happiness', _dHappy], ['Relationship', _dRel], ['Behavior', _dBehavior]])>><</linkdesc>>
			<</if>>
		<</replace>>
	<</link>>
	<</if>>
	<<if _canTutor>>
		<<if _tutorBlocked>>
			<div>Help with tutoring [<<=_tutorBlocked>>]</div>
		<<else>>
			<<link 'Help with tutoring (Uses 1 hour)'>>
				<<replace '#nce_choices'>>
					<<include 'NPC offer nursery help - tutor subject'>>
				<</replace>>
			<</link>>
		<</if>>
	<</if>>
	<<if _canDrink>>
		<<if setup.cabinInventory.has('energy_drink')>>
			<<link `'Give ' + _himOrHer + ' an energy drink (Uses 1 energy drink)'`>>
				<<replace '#nce_choices'>>
					<<run _help.markHelped(_carer)>>
					<<run setup.cabinInventory.drop('energy_drink')>>
					<<set _dRel = _help.change(_carer, 'relationship', 1, 2)>>
					<<set _dHappy = _help.change(_carer, 'happy', 2, 4)>>
					<<set _dBehavior = _help.change(_care, 'behavior', 1, 3)>>
					You hand <<=_himOrHer>> a can. <<=_name>> cracks it open, drinks half of it in one go, and closes <<=_hisOrHer>> eyes for a moment.
					<br /><br />
					<<sayNpc _carer>>
						Oh, I needed that. Thank you. All right, who's next?
					<</sayNpc>>
					<br />
					<<=ucfirst(_heOrShe)>> goes back to the kids with a spring in <<=_hisOrHer>> step, and they seem to pick up on it. For a while, the room runs a good deal more smoothly.
					<br /><br />
					<<link 'Continue'>><<goto _backPassage>><</link>>
					<<linkdesc>><<=_extra.effects([['Happiness', _dHappy], ['Relationship', _dRel], ['Behavior', _dBehavior]])>><</linkdesc>>
				<</replace>>
			<</link>>
		<<else>>
			<div>Give <<=_himOrHer>> an energy drink [Need energy drink]</div>
		<</if>>
		<<if _canPrep>>
		<<elseif setup.cabinInventory.has('energy_drink', 2)>>
			<<link 'Have an energy drink together (Uses 2 energy drinks and 30 minutes)'>>
				<<replace '#nce_choices'>>
					<<run _help.markHelped(_carer)>>
					<<run _help.consume({ energy_drink: 2 })>>
					<<addmins 30>>
					<<energy 10>>
					<<set _dRel = _help.change(_carer, 'relationship', 1, 2)>>
					<<set _dRel += _help.change(_carer, 'relationship', 1, 3)>>
					<<set _dHappy = _help.change(_carer, 'happy', 2, 4)>>
					<<set _dBehavior = _help.change(_care, 'behavior', 1, 3)>>
					You hand <<=_himOrHer>> a can and open one for yourself. The two of you sit on the low bench by the window, knees up around your ears on furniture built for much smaller people, and <<=_name>> tells you about the kids: who has started reading, who isn't speaking to whom, and who has decided that peas are poison.
					<br /><br />
					<<sayNpc _carer>>
						I can't remember the last time I sat down and talked to someone taller than my waist. Thank you. I mean that. I'll be good for the rest of the day now.
					<</sayNpc>>
					<br />
					<<=ucfirst(_heOrShe)>> gives your arm a squeeze and heads back in, and the kids settle quickly under <<=_hisOrHer>> renewed good humor.
					<br /><br />
					<<link 'Continue'>><<goto _backPassage>><</link>>
					<<linkdesc>><<=_extra.effects([['Happiness', _dHappy], ['Relationship', _dRel], ['Behavior', _dBehavior]])>><</linkdesc>>
					<<linkdesc>>Your energy +10<</linkdesc>>
				<</replace>>
			<</link>>
		<<else>>
			<div>Have an energy drink together [Need 2 energy drinks]</div>
		<</if>>
	<</if>>
	<<if _canPrep>>
	<<elseif _toyMissing.length < 1>>
		<<link 'Help make toys (Uses 1 plastic, 1 cloth, 1 glue, 1 wood and 1 hour)'>>
			<<replace '#nce_choices'>>
				<<run _help.markHelped(_carer)>>
				<<run _help.consume(_toyNeeds)>>
				<<addmins 60>>
				<<set _smarts = _help.intBonus()>>
				<<set _isMechanic = (_carer.skills ?? []).includes('mechanic')>>
				<<set _isInventive = setup.personality.has(_carer, 'inventive')>>
				<<set _made = random(1, 3) + _smarts + (_isMechanic || _isInventive ? 5 : 0)>>
				<<set _care.toys += _made>>
				<<set _dHappy = _help.change(_carer, 'happy', 1, 2)>>
				<<set _dRel = _help.change(_carer, 'relationship', 2, 4)>>
				<<set _dBehavior = 0>>
				You spread the materials out on the long table and settle in with <<=_name>>. <<=ucfirst(_heOrShe)>> stitches while you cut and glue, and the two of you turn out handmade toys: a block set, a jointed doll, a rattle that rattles more than it should. A few of the kids drift over to supervise and offer strong opinions.
				<<if _isMechanic || _isInventive>>
					<<if _isMechanic && _isInventive>>
						<<=_name>>'s experience in keeping engines running, and <<=_hisOrHer>> talent for finding inventive solutions, both show.
					<<elseif _isMechanic>>
						<<=_name>>'s experience in keeping engines running shows.
					<<else>>
						<<=_name>>'s talent for finding inventive solutions shows.
					<</if>>
					<<=ucfirst(_heOrShe)>> fits pieces together with hinges and pegs instead of extra glue, salvages offcuts you would have thrown away, and builds the toys sturdy enough to survive the kids. You end up with far more than you planned on, and they're better made, too.
				<</if>>
				<<if _smarts>>
					You work out a way to get extra pieces from the same scraps, and <<=_name>> gives you an impressed look.
				<</if>>
				<br /><br />
				<<sayNpc _carer>>
					This was nice. I don't get to make things very often, I'm usually just fixing them. Thank you for spending the time with me. They're going to love these.
				<</sayNpc>>
				<br />
				<strong>(Toys +<<=_made>>)</strong>
				<br /><br />
				<<link 'Continue'>><<goto _backPassage>><</link>>
				<<linkdesc>><<=_extra.effects([['Happiness', _dHappy], ['Relationship', _dRel], ['Behavior', _dBehavior]])>><</linkdesc>>
			<</replace>>
		<</link>>
	<<else>>
		<div>Help make toys [Need <<=_toyMissing.join(', ')>>]</div>
	<</if>>
	<<if _canPrep>>
	<<elseif _snackMissing.length < 1>>
		<<link 'Help make healthy snacks (Uses 2 food, 1 plastic and 1 hour)'>>
			<<replace '#nce_choices'>>
				<<run _help.markHelped(_carer)>>
				<<run _help.consume(_snackNeeds)>>
				<<addmins 60>>
				<<set _smarts = _help.intBonus()>>
				<<set _isCook = (_carer.skills ?? []).includes('cook')>>
				<<set _made = random(5, 10) + _smarts + (_isCook ? 5 : 0)>>
				<<set _care.snacks += _made>>
				<<set _dHappy = _help.change(_carer, 'happy', 1, 2)>>
				<<set _dRel = _help.change(_carer, 'relationship', 2, 4)>>
				<<set _dBehavior = 0>>
				<<set _learnedCook = _help.addCookExperience(_carer)>>
				You bring over some fruit and dried meat from the kitchen, and the two of you set up at the long table, slicing and portioning it out into small wrapped packets.
				<<if _isCook>>
					<<=_name>>'s kitchen experience shows. <<=ucfirst(_heOrShe)>> gets more out of the materials than you would have thought possible, with hardly a scrap wasted, and the snacks come out better for it.
				<</if>>
				<<if _smarts>>
					You suggest a better way to portion it all out, and it stretches the batch a little further.
				<</if>>
				<br /><br />
				<<sayNpc _carer>>
					Thank you for this. A little something between meals keeps them from getting cranky, and it's good for them, too.<<if !_isCook>> I learned a thing or two about stretching a batch, too.<</if>>
				<</sayNpc>>
				<br />
				<strong>(Snacks +<<=_made>>)</strong>
				<<if _learnedCook>>
					<br />
					<span class="skill"><<=_carer.name>> learned the cook skill</span>
				<</if>>
				<br /><br />
				<<link 'Continue'>><<goto _backPassage>><</link>>
				<<linkdesc>><<=_extra.effects([['Happiness', _dHappy], ['Relationship', _dRel], ['Behavior', _dBehavior]])>><</linkdesc>>
			<</replace>>
		<</link>>
	<<else>>
		<div>Help make healthy snacks [Need <<=_snackMissing.join(', ')>>]</div>
	<</if>>
	<<link 'Maybe another time'>>
		<<replace '#nce_choices'>>
			You tell <<=_himOrHer>> you'll have to come back to it when you have what you need.
			<br /><br />
			<<sayNpc _carer>>
				I'll hold you to that. Thanks for saying what you did, though.
			<</sayNpc>>
			<br />
			<<link 'Continue'>><<goto _backPassage>><</link>>
		<</replace>>
	<</link>>
</div>


:: NPC offer nursery help - tutor subject
/* Tutoring, step one: pick a subject. Included inside #nce_choices by NPC offer nursery help. */
<<set _subjects = _extra.subjects()>>
<<set _tutorSubject = _subjects[0]>>
<<set _useBook = false>>
<<you>>
	The school-age ones are back from class. I could sit down with a couple of them and go over their lessons.
<</you>>
<br />
<<sayNpc _carer>>
	Would you? Some of them are falling behind, and I never have the time to sit with them properly. Which subject?
<</sayNpc>>
<br />
<<for _subject range _subjects>>
	<<set _count = _extra.students(_subject).length>>
	<label><<radiobutton "_tutorSubject" _subject autocheck>> <<=_subject>> <small>(<<=_count>> <<=_count === 1 ? 'student' : 'students'>>)</small></label>
	<br />
<</for>>
<br />
<<if setup.cabinInventory.has('book')>>
	<label><<checkbox "_useBook" false true autocheck>> Bring a book along (Uses 1 book, +<<=_extra.BOOK_BONUS>> skill points each)</label>
<<else>>
	<span style="opacity: 0.6">Bring a book along [Need book]</span>
<</if>>
<br /><br />
<<link 'Choose the students'>>
	<<replace '#nce_choices'>>
		<<include 'NPC offer nursery help - tutor students'>>
	<</replace>>
<</link>>
<<link 'Never mind'>>
	<<replace '#nce_choices'>>
		You think better of it and tell <<=_himOrHer>> you'll have to come back to it another day.
		<br /><br />
		<<link 'Continue'>><<goto _backPassage>><</link>>
	<</replace>>
<</link>>


:: NPC offer nursery help - tutor students
/* Tutoring, step two: pick up to two students, then the lesson itself */
<<set _students = _extra.students(_tutorSubject)>>
<<set _tutorPicks = {}>>
Who would you like to sit down with for <strong><<=_tutorSubject>></strong>? You can take up to <<=_extra.TUTOR_MAX_STUDENTS>>.
<br /><br />
<<for _i, _student range _students>>
	<label>
		<<checkbox `"_tutorPicks['" + _student.id + "']"` false true>>
		<span @class="'gender-' + setup.genderClass(_student)"><<=_student.name>></span>, age <<=setup.getAge(_student)>>
		<small>(<<=_student.teach && _student.teach !== 'random' ? 'studying ' + _student.teach : 'studying at random'>>, <<=Math.min(100, _extra.progress(_student, _tutorSubject))>>/100)</small>
	</label>
	<br />
<</for>>
<br />
<span id="nce_tutor_note"></span>
<<link 'Start the lesson'>>
	<<set _chosen = _students.filter(k => _tutorPicks[k.id])>>
	<<if _chosen.length < 1>>
		<<replace '#nce_tutor_note'>><span style="color: orange">Pick at least one student.</span><br /><br /><</replace>>
	<<elseif _chosen.length > _extra.TUTOR_MAX_STUDENTS>>
		<<replace '#nce_tutor_note'>><span style="color: orange">You can only take <<=_extra.TUTOR_MAX_STUDENTS>> at a time.</span><br /><br /><</replace>>
	<<else>>
		<<run _help.markHelped(_carer)>>
		<<addmins 60>>
		<<set _gained = _extra.tutor(_chosen, _tutorSubject, _useBook)>>
		<<set _dBehavior = _help.change(_care, 'behavior', 3, 5)>>
		<<set _dHappy = _help.change(_carer, 'happy', 2, 4)>>
		<<set _dRel = _help.change(_carer, 'relationship', 2, 4)>>
		<<set _chosenNames = _chosen.map(k => k.name).join(' and ')>>
		<<replace '#nce_choices'>>
			You clear a corner of the long table and sit down with <<=_chosenNames>>.
			<<if _useBook>>
				You open the book between you and work through it page by page, stopping whenever something needs explaining a second way.
			<<else>>
				You go over what they covered in class and fill in what they missed, one question at a time.
			<</if>>
			<<if _chosen.length > 1>>
				By the end of the hour they are finishing each other's answers, and arguing about who got there first.
			<<else>>
				By the end of the hour, <<=_chosen[0].name>> is answering before you finish asking.
			<</if>>
			<br /><br />
			Around the room the other kids have been quieter than usual, as if school had followed them home.
			<br /><br />
			<<sayNpc _carer>>
				Thank you. That's an hour of teaching I didn't have to find time for. And did you see their faces when they got it right?
			<</sayNpc>>
			<br />
			<<for _student range _chosen>>
				<strong>(<<=_student.name>>: <<=_tutorSubject>> +<<=_gained.points[_student.id]>>)</strong>
				<<if _extra.progress(_student, _tutorSubject) >= 100>>
					<small>Ready to learn the <<=_tutorSubject>> skill tonight.</small>
				<</if>>
				<br />
			<</for>>
			<<if _useBook>>
				<small>(Used 1 book)</small>
				<br />
			<</if>>
			<br />
			<<link 'Continue'>><<goto _backPassage>><</link>>
			<<linkdesc>><<=_extra.effects([['Happiness', _dHappy], ['Relationship', _dRel], ['Behavior', _dBehavior]])>><</linkdesc>>
			<<for _student range _chosen>>
				<<linkdesc>><<=_extra.effects([['Relationship', _gained.relationship[_student.id]]], _student.name)>><</linkdesc>>
			<</for>>
		<</replace>>
	<</if>>
<</link>>
<<link 'Never mind'>>
	<<replace '#nce_choices'>>
		You think better of it and tell <<=_himOrHer>> you'll have to come back to it another day.
		<br /><br />
		<<link 'Continue'>><<goto _backPassage>><</link>>
	<</replace>>
<</link>>
